PROSE-TRAP GUARD: give a recorded vision the same 'where it breaks' contract bibliothecaire already enforces on briefs. Origin: 2026-07-28 interactive /ideate, Zach-directed, after this session wrote a 100-line 'The long arc' vision section into scheduler's FOCUS.md, then in the SAME session described bibliothecaire's --require-briefs as the guard against prose that 'makes the machinery feel understood' -- and did not apply it to its own output. Nothing caught this; Zach did. THE GAP, probed not assumed: (a) .claude/commands/ideate.md step 4's standard entry shape is vision / milestone chain / blockers -- grep for break|disanalogy|falsif|counter over that file returns ZERO hits, so there is no slot in the prescribed shape for what would make the vision wrong; (b) bin/validate-quotes.py --require-briefs enforces exactly that slot, but is scoped to bibliothecaire/briefs/ and does not read FOCUS.md prose anywhere; (c) hygiene-lint.sh checks stamped-checklist drift and stale 'verified <date>' claims but nothing about a vision entry's content; closeout-lint.sh checks durability (dirty tree, unpushed commit, sha-less session record) but not honesty. So a vision entry is the least-checked prose in the ecosystem while being the most load-bearing -- everything downstream triages against it. PROPOSAL, two halves, do the mechanical one first: (1) LINT: extend hygiene-lint.sh (or a new vision-lint) to FLAG any FOCUS.md section matching the /ideate vision shape that lacks a named falsifier -- the roadmap analogue of 'where it breaks' is 'what would make this wrong', plus the two properties tonight's entry does already have but which are unenforced: every milestone step names what it RETIRES, and every 'decided' claim carries a date + provenance. Same offline-first, signals-not-verdicts, exit-0 stance as the other four surveys. (2) PROSE: add the falsifier slot to ideate.md step 4's standard entry shape so it is prescribed, not just linted. Do (1) first and deliberately: fixing prose decay with more prose is the recursion this whole finding is about, and the lint is the half that survives being forgotten. NOTE the honest limit, stated rather than smoothed over: a lint can check that a falsifier EXISTS, not that it is a good one -- same limit --require-briefs has, and it was still worth building there.
